---
import Article from "../components/article.astro";
import { Definition } from "../components/definition";
import Heading from "../components/heading.astro";
import { CompleteSentence } from "../components/morphology/complete-sentence";
import { SetConlangName } from "../components/morphology/set-conlang-name";
import Paragraph from "../components/paragraph.astro";
import { Popup } from "../components/popup";
import { WaitUntil } from "../components/wait-until";
import { PAGES } from "../constants";
import SectionLayout from "../layouts/section.astro";
---

<SectionLayout title="Morphology" position="1" nextPath="/morphemes">
  <Article page="morphology" progress={2} nextPath="/morphemes">
    <Heading>Hello!</Heading>

    <Paragraph>Welcome to <i>remorphable.</i></Paragraph>

    <Paragraph>
      Through this interactive web experience, you will get an introduction to
      <b>morphology</b>, one of the many fascinating branches of
      <Popup client:load triggerText="linguistics">
        <p>
          Linguistics is the scientific study of language, including learning
          how it is structured, developed, and used.
        </p>
        <p>
          It does <b>not</b> mean learning a specific language or languages (a common
          misconception), although linguists <i>may</i> end up learning languages
          that they are analyzing.
        </p>
      </Popup>. You will learn about some of the basic concepts in morphology,
      including morphemes, lexemes, allomorphy, inflection, derivation, and
      more. These words may sound intimidating at the moment, but fret not! Very
      soon you’ll have a good understanding of what each of these concepts mean
      and why they’re important.
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph>
      The fun part? <b>You’ll get to build your own language!</b> Incorporating what
      you learn at each stage, you’ll gradually give shape to a
      <Popup client:load triggerText="conlang">
        <p>
          A conlang (short for constructed language) is an artificial language
          that is specifically created by a person or group, rather than
          naturally emerging from human interaction.
        </p>
        <p>Some famous examples of conlangs include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href="https://www.babbel.com/en/magazine/klingon-language"
              >Klingon</a
            >, from Star Trek, developed by a linguist to be intentionally
            distinct from human languages through the use of rare linguistic
            features.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newspeak">Newspeak</a>, from
            the novel 1984. Although this conlang is similar to English, it
            features a much more simplified vocabulary and restricted grammar,
            made to limit free thought and personal expression.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Popup>
      that gets more morphologically rich as you progress through the website.
    </Paragraph>

    <SetConlangName client:load />

    <WaitUntil progress={1} page={PAGES.morphology} client:idle>
      <Paragraph>Now, then...</Paragraph>

      <Heading>What exactly is morphology?</Heading>

      <Paragraph>
        Let’s say we have the following (imaginary) English word:
      </Paragraph>

      <Definition
        term="splinch"
        category="verb"
        ipa="splɪntʃ"
        definition="to step on broken glass"
      />

      <Paragraph>Try to use this word in the following sentence:</Paragraph>

      <CompleteSentence client:idle />
    </WaitUntil>

    <WaitUntil progress={2} page={PAGES.morphology} client:idle>
      <Paragraph>
        If you entered <b>“splinched”</b>, then this demonstrates that you have
        an intuitive knowledge of how to form new words in English, even if you
        have never used or heard of them before. We never defined the past tense
        of “splinch” as “splinched,” and yet you inferred that adding the suffix
        “-ed” to the word would change its form and make it appropriate to use
        in this sentence.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        How did you know that adding “-ed” has this effect? In fact, what other
        rules and techniques exist that help you modify words to convey
        different meanings? <i>How exactly are words formed?</i>
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        Morphology is the branch of linguistics that deals with this sort of
        thing. It is <b>the study of word formation</b>.
      </Paragraph>

      <Definition
        term="morphology"
        category="noun"
        ipa="mɔɹˈfɑ.lə.dʒi"
        definition="a study of structure and form; in linguistics, it is the study and description of word formation in language"
      />
    </WaitUntil>
  </Article>
</SectionLayout>
